text:
Key takeaways

Most RBPs have a clear main binding region. 206 of 231 experiments favour one region group:

CDS 44, 3′UTR 35, 5′UTR 13;
ncRNA 33;
splice-site intron 28, deep intron 53.

Proteins with known roles land where expected (e.g. RBFOX2 and U2AF2 near splice sites, PUM2 and UPF1 in 3′UTRs).
Binding location points to function:

splice-site and deep-intron binders: pre-mRNA processing;
3′UTR binders: mRNA stability;
5′UTR binders: translation;
ncRNA binders: small-RNA processing.

Binding profiles form a continuum, not discrete classes. Two axes, introns vs coding sequence and 5′UTR/small RNA vs 3′UTR, explain 56% of the variation; clustering found no natural groups.
A binding profile is a property of the protein. The same RBP’s K562 and HepG2 profiles correlate with median r = 0.89 (different RBPs: -0.01), and their distance is less than half that between different RBPs (1.54 vs 3.50, p &lt; 0.001).
